hat you need to do and if  you have a question don't be shy about  calling Todd or I or any of the other  participants early participants of the  national network yeah I mean just just  following up on what Bobby said you know  the national network options  considerations document is an incredible  place to start you oftentimes running  that first paragraph of a paper is the  heart of thing in many ways that's  already been done for you to really look  at how you can use the templates and the  guidance and contextualize it for your  locale the other thing I would say is  don't do it in the back room of the  state agency or in the conference room  of your local nonprofit you think about  this as a true your multi stakeholder  effort that is likely the only way that  you're going to get the diversifying  that you're going to need not just for  that first trade but for a long-standing  sustainable endeavor it's much better to  understand the concern and the risks  from the agricultural community the  environmental sector up front and try to  work through them and and you know sink  through the pros and cons a different  approaches early on then to spend  five hundred thousand dollars in 18  months and then realized that the way  you've been approaching things may not  fly because of the real you know  concerns of one sector or another so  there is the content and there is the  regulatory processes and considerations  but like most environmental issues you  know that there's a real social  component as well and don't undersell  the time and effort it will take to  bring people together towards these  common goals and Todd that this reminded  me to that anytime you can kind of grab  a buddy I think we're we're seeing a lot  of benefit if you're wanting to start up  a trading program of grabbing a  neighboring state and doing it at the  same time or at least grabbing a couple  watersheds in within the same state and  doing it all at the same time because  you can take your lessons together and  get this stuff going faster and cheaper  and then I think the other ask would be  if you're if you're noodling on  something and having a hard time getting  through an issue don't be shy about  asking asking for help there's a there's  a lot of people now that are better  connected than they ever were that can  provide some of those those lessons  learned yes sounds great and that  concludes the talk on water quality  markets once again we would like to  thank Todd Gartner and Bobby Cochran for  joining us today if you wish to view a  recording of this webinar please visit  the Yale Center for business and the  environment website or access the  recording to the Yale iTunes University  podcast thank you for joining us until  next time this is andrew woo from the  yale center for business and the  environment in collaboration with the  conservation finance network
 

